  urldate = {2026-11-11},
  abstract = {Hallucination in large language models poses a substantial barrier to their reliable application in high-stakes engineering and decision-making domains. Existing post-hoc correction methods, such as self-reflection and agent-based debate, offer partial relief but often suffer from convergence to flawed consensus, or adversarial degeneration. In this study, we propose a novel debate framework inspired by the classical Socratic method, which emphasizes critical questioning and conceptual clarification as a means of improving reasoning accuracy. The proposed system organizes multiple language model agents into structured roles and orchestrates a three-phase interaction comprising initial answer generation, iterative Socratic questioning, and final judgment. Different from previous adversarial debates, our method encourages epistemic transparency by guiding agents to challenge assumptions, examine vague definitions, and resolve inconsistencies through structured dialogue. Experimental results on both open-source and commercial language models show consistent improvements in factual accuracy over strong baselines, including recent multi-agent debate methods. The proposed framework offers a lightweight, model-agnostic, and interpretable solution to hallucination mitigation, with potential applications in knowledge-intensive artificial intelligence systems where trust and reliability are paramount.},
