  abstract = {Technology evaluation underpins resource allocation in innovation systems, yet expert assessments are costly, show limited inter-rater reliability, and are seldom validated against ex-post firm outcomes. Before asking whether a large language model (LLM) judge is accurate, we ask whether it is reliable. We retrospectively audit such a judge on 9,926 Korean SMEs evaluated by a public technology-credit institution and linked to their registry operating status in June 2023 (529 verified failure events—bankruptcy, dissolution, closure, or court-supervised rehabilitation—a prevalence of 5.3\%). The judge is highly repeatable: at temperature 0 it re-scores byte-identical descriptions with a single-rating test–retest ICC of 0.995 (0.986 under stochastic sampling), which is within-model repeatability, not inter-rater agreement. Its criterionrelated validity is modest: within an expert tier, the LLM, a TF-IDF baseline, multilingual embeddings, and the expert grade reach an observed 0.57–0.60 AUROC plateau against later operating status, paired tests detect no clear difference among the leading methods, and neither prompt engineering nor enabling model reasoning raises it. Concordance with expert grades is governed chiefly by industry category: the LLM ranks technologies well within a sector but miscalibrates across sectors, so it does not exceed a trivial industry-code baseline overall, although, without labels, it matches a text model trained on one-to-two thousand institutional labels on top-tier detection. The scores can order a review queue, but cross-validated thresholds support neither automatic acceptance nor rejection at conventional purity targets. The judge’s value lies in reproducible, auditable, standardized scoring within a human-in-the-loop pipeline, not in outcome prediction.},
