  urldate = {2026-11-11},
  abstract = {Large Language Model-based agents have demonstrated impressive capabilities across various domains. To further enhance their abilities, the collaboration of multiple agents presents a promising avenue. Recently, Multi-Agent Debate (MAD) was introduced as a representative collaborative paradigm, wherein agents engage in multiple rounds of discussion to explore potential solutions to a problem. However, researchers observed that MAD is not stably superior to single-agent methods. Unfortunately, there has been insufficient investigation of this issue. In light of this limitation, we for the first time empirically identify what leads to the instability of MAD: the woozle effect, which refers to the propagation of hallucinations among agents during debate. Because MAD employs a static and fully connected communication topology, agents are constrained to passively receive information from all connected peers, which increases the risk of being misled by hallucinations and subsequent propagation. To mitigate this challenge, we propose DIGRA, a novel MAD framework with Dynamic communication topology driven by the Information Gain RAtio. Our evaluations across various benchmarks demonstrate that appropriately selecting communication partners significantly mitigates hallucination propagation and yields superior collective intelligence.},
