  abstract = {Self-consistency (SC) improves chain-of-thought prompting by sampling multiple reasoning paths and voting, but it often requires many samples. Confidence-Informed Self-Consistency (CISC) reduces this cost by weighted voting using an outcome-confidence signal such as P(True); for physics, this signal can overweight confident but flawed derivations. We propose PhysCISC, a process-credibility weighted self-consistency framework for physics reason ing. PhysCISC retains the same inference-time aggregation structure as CISC while replacing outcome-confidence weights with a rubric-derived process-credibility score for the observable reasoning chain. We evaluate three open-source LLMs: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, and Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3. We also build a Chinese physics multiple-choice benchmark of 3848 questions (six domains; five reasoning types) curated from printed problem books. On this main set, PhysCISC is broadly comparable to CISC overall (48.98\% vs. 48.81\%), with model- and budget-dependent gains and losses. Signal-quality analyses show that process-credibility and 𝑃( True) are complementary rather than redundant: a same-pool two-signal diagnostic indicates non-overlap be tween the two signals, and difficulty/domain/type decompositions show distinct subsets where each signal is preferable. We further evaluate on three public physics benchmarks converted to the same four-option format: UGPhysics NV\&EX (600), SciBench-Physics (267), and MMLU-Physics (488); public-benchmark transfer is mixed, indicating that process-credibility is a complementary process-oriented scoring signal rather than a universal re placement for the outcome-confidence signal. A limited manual reasoning-chain inspection and process-shape ablations further suggest that process-credibility is not reducible to answer length or step count. The dataset and reproducible code are released on Zenodo.},
  langid = {english},
