  urldate = {2026-11-11},
  abstract = {Large language models (LLMs) are widely used to score outputs, but agreement with a human scale and reliable selection among alternatives are different targets. We use GraphCal-Judge, a fold-local relational calibrator, to ask when relational calibration helps an LLM-as-a-judge. An exploratory analysis of 848 SM-RS participant ratings and 1,600 SummEval expert-rated summaries, scored by four LLM judges under strict-inductive splits, suggested three propositions: relational calibration lowers error while neighborhood identities persist (H1); a pairwise head helps selection only when within-cell contrasts persist (H2); and affine correction transfers across corpora better than relational structure (H3). Tree regressors matched or beat GraphCal-Judge in MAE on both corpora. We then tested H1–H3 on HANNA story ratings under a protocol deposited before any rating was read. Two predictions held: relational calibration, like ridge and random forest, lowered leave-prompt MAE below affine correction (gain 0.059, 95\% interval [0.044, 0.073]), and in both leave-corpus directions into HANNA, affine correction reduced MAE while relational ranking did not improve selection. Two did not: label-free diagnostics did not predict fold-level gains, and the pairwise head added nothing over the value head. On these corpora, value calibration under stable identities and affine transport replicate; dedicated pairwise heads do not.},
